# Local, machine-specific configuration (never commit endpoints/secrets).
minerva.local.toml
.env

# Training artefacts. The rule: anything large and regenerable stays out; the
# small files that *describe* a model - and that a reader needs to retrain it
# or audit a claim - stay in. A fresh clone must be able to rebuild the corpus
# and retrain from source, which is why the recipe is committed and only the
# output is not.
#
# Out: the corpus text (gigabytes, downloaded by `minerva prepare-data` and
# never vendored - see src/minerva/training/data.py), packed token arrays,
# per-experiment eval snapshots, and checkpoint weights, which belong in a
# release rather than in git history.
data/*.txt
data/*.bin
data/*.json
data/*.log
data/cache/
data_v*/*.txt
data_v*/*.bin
data_v*/*.json
data_v*/cache/
checkpoints/
*.pt

# In. gutenberg_bulk_ids.json (40 KB) names the exact ebooks in the bulk
# corpus: without it a fresh clone gets a *similar* corpus, not the same one,
# because the fetcher walks a seed-shuffled catalogue and what lands on disk
# also depends on transient fetch failures and the stub threshold.
# manifest.json carries each source's licence, character counts and SHA-256 -
# the provenance CLAUDE.md requires. The two eval reports are the measured
# numbers the README and the DEVDEBUG panel quote, and a claim with no
# committed measurement behind it is what this project exists to avoid; the
# other instruct_eval_*.json files are one-off experiment snapshots and stay
# out. tokenizer.json pins how text maps to ids for a given checkpoint.
!data/gutenberg_bulk_ids.json
!data/manifest.json
!data/tokenizer.json
!data/eval_report.json
!data/instruct_eval_report.json
!data_v*/manifest.json
!data_v*/tokenizer.json
!data/tokenizer.json
!data_v*/manifest.json
!data_v*/tokenizer.json

# Scratch downloads of CI artifacts, used to compare a hosted run against
# the local one (see CLAUDE.md section 8). Not source.
ci_artifacts/
ci_reports/

eval_root/
checkpoints/snapshot/
eval_combo/
